  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nMid/upper water vapor loops this afternoon show the broad\nridge finally being shunted farther west into the Plains as a\ntrough deepens across the Great Lakes and Northeast U.S. This\nwill bring the much anticipated airmass change by this evening\nand tonight as Canadian high pressure at the surface builds down\nacross the Great Lakes. A secondary cold front currently sinking\nsouth and west through the area is advecting in the lower dew\npoints, with surface observations falling into the 50s in far NE\nOhio and NW PA. These lower dew points will continue to spread\nsouth and west behind the front through the evening.\n\nThe drier air and high pressure will allow for a pleasantly \ncool night, with lows in the low/mid 50s away from the lake.\nThis will set up a much different day Sunday, with skies\nbecoming mostly sunny and highs only in the mid/upper 70s. The\none caveat to the cooler, quieter weather is growing potential \nfor lake-effect rain showers tonight through Sunday morning. NE \nboundary layer flow will lead to bands of showers impacting NW \nand north central Ohio, especially between the islands and \nToledo, but areas immediately near the lakeshore from Lorain to \nErie, PA could be clipped by brief showers as well. Increased \nPOPS for this potential late tonight into Sunday morning. The \nlong northeasterly fetch and 850 mb temps falling below 10 C \nwill support a few hundred joules of lake induced CAPE, and low-\nlevel convergence will also aid in band development, so \nconfidence is increasing for showers. Relatively shallow low- \nlevel moisture should at least keep the activity fairly light. \nOtherwise, the seasonably cool and dry weather will continue \nthrough Labor Day Monday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nThe large Canadian high will drift into the Mid Atlantic Tuesday\nand Tuesday night as the central CONUS mid/upper ridge once\nagain expands east into the Ohio and Tennessee Valleys. This\nwill set up return southerly flow and advect the hot and humid\nair back into the area for midweek. Highs Tuesday will get back\ninto the low/mid 80s with a noticeable increase in humidity, and\nsome 90s are possible Wednesday as the heat and humidity becomes\nfirmly reestablished. Shortwave energy topping the building \nridge Tuesday night/Wednesday morning combined with a low-level\njet impinging on the lifting warm front could spark some showers\nand thunderstorms in NE Ohio and NW PA, but left things dry at \nthis time. Better potential for showers and thunderstorms will \ncome late Wednesday and Wednesday night as a strong northern\nstream mid/upper shortwave trough digs into the Great Lakes with\na deep associated surface low progressing through northern\nOntario and Quebec. This will push a cold front through the\nregion Wednesday night. Depending on timing of the frontal\npassage, strong to severe storms will be possible since jet\ndynamics look impressive. The front will be slow to push south,\nso expect showers to continue Thursday, especially south of a\nFindlay to Youngstown line before Canadian high pressure returns\nFriday and Saturday. So, after the brief midweek heat and storms,\ncooler, drier, and less humid air will return Thursday through \nSaturday.",
